														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2692719">
									

<p>The one thing I want to point out about this is how it uses AWCY. They're anartists, but usually, we aren't looking at them as people who actually think about their art. We usually see dangerous people trying to make a point, cause a panic, and say, &quot;that's art!&quot; That has the same effectiveness as spreading hate and then saying, &quot;But I'm only joking!&quot;</p>
<p>But here, we don't have that. We have someone who is frustrated with the natural problem in art, namely <em>how do you craft a message and ensure that it is effectively received?</em> A viewer's interpretation of the art is fully dependent on the viewer's experiences, and so the artist's emotional thrust can be utterly lost to the viewer. Every author here, whether their articles succeed or get deleted, knows this struggle.</p>
<p>So can that be subverted? Must the artwork end at the viewer's judgment of it? Or can the judgment of that art become part of the art itself? Can the artist elicit the feeling in the viewer directly, and not rely on ever changing cultural markers and interpretations and experiences inherent in people the artist will never meet or know to effect its impact? This is one anartist's attempt to answer that question.</p>
<p>And <em>that</em> is how you ask if we're cool yet.</p>

							</div>

								
					
