<blockquote>
<p>the implication I'm getting is &quot;a memetic family&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not necessarily. Remember that we don't know whether or not the entity, SCP-2708-A, actually exists - even anomalously/non-corporeally - or is strictly a hallucination brought on by the baseline cognitohazardous effects tied to viewing Figure A. Or maybe it's both, because what is reality? Is reality what we perceive or what others perceive? If you yourself feel and see something as being real, and you believe it's real, couldn't you argue that it <em>is</em> real, even if observers disagree? Are the baseline parameters of reality personal or public? Things like that.</p>
<p>So no, it's not an entire family by concept, it's the effect of convincing oneself that one has a partner and is able to proceed down typical life paths with said partner (yes, including making a family, in that one instance) - but it can only go so far, and it depends on the extent to which the person affected believes the events to be true. That's why amnesticization usually fixes the problem, if not removing the conceptual memories entirely.</p>

